        "model": "GPT-6.1"
      },
      "text": "Siloli Desert, colourful lagoons and Uyuni"
    },
    {
      "blockId": "tour:itinerary_text:2e780f63f4172747",
      "field": "itinerary.text",
      "sourceTextSha256": "c7c29d314be156eaef04d1b26ae52e932aeb80c580196ea77ee897ce317289a7",
      "provenance": {
        "kind": "ai_translation",
        "runId": "ada-en-20261110-51o02k29bbyeytxstzb7f4",
        "model": "GPT-6.1"
      },
      "text": "We set off at around 06:00. Your first stop is the Stone Tree, a famous rock formation in the Siloli Desert. From there, you continue to Laguna Colorada, known for its unusually coloured waters and flamingo colonies.\n\nThe next stop is Sol de Mañana, where volcanic activity sends steam through fumaroles and geysers. The route then takes you to the Polques Hot Springs, across the Dalí Desert with its wind-sculpted stone columns, and on to Laguna Verde at the foot of Licancabur Volcano.\n\nOn the way back to Uyuni, you walk through the Valley of the Rocks near Alota, where huge volcanic boulders lie scattered across the landscape. Your final stop is San Cristóbal, a village relocated from its original site to make way for one of Bolivia's largest mines. You reach Uyuni at around 18:00–19:00 and spend the night in a hotel built from salt.\n\nIn a single day, the landscape shifts from stony desert to a red lagoon, steaming vents and boiling ground in the geyser field, hot springs, green waters beneath Licancabur and giant volcanic boulders in the Valley of the Rocks. The altitude and wide-open terrain make this one of the most unusual stretches of the trip."
    },
